## Exercise 1 — a slot instead of a flag

Edit `ex1/solution.tsx`. The stub exports a `Card` with a configuration-shaped
API: `showFooter`, `footerText` and `footerAlign`. Replace those three props
with one slot.

Export:

```tsx
export function Card(props: {
  title: string;
  children: ReactNode;
  footer?: ReactNode;
}): ReactElement;
```

Rendering rules:

- an `<article>` wrapping an `<h3>` with the title and a `<div>` with class
  `card-body` containing `children`;
- when `footer` is supplied, a `<footer>` with class `card-footer` containing
  it, after the body;
- when `footer` is **not** supplied, no `<footer>` element at all — not an
  empty one.

"Supplied" means anything other than `undefined`. The gate renders
`<Card title="T" footer={0}>x</Card>` and expects a footer containing `0`, and
`<Card title="T" footer="">x</Card>` and expects an empty footer element to
exist. A truthiness check fails both.

```
uv run dojo test react v3rc01_component_api_design ex1
```

references.solution.tsx:
// v3rc01_component_api_design / ex1 - reference solution.
import type { ReactElement, ReactNode } from "react";

export function Card({
  title,
  children,
  footer,
}: {
  title: string;
  children: ReactNode;
  footer?: ReactNode;
}): ReactElement {
  return (
    <article>
      <h3>{title}</h3>
      <div className="card-body">{children}</div>
      {/* `!= null` and not `&&`: an empty string and the number 0 are both
          legitimate content a caller may want in the footer, and `&&` would
          drop the first and print the second as a stray "0". */}
      {footer != null && <footer className="card-footer">{footer}</footer>}
    </article>
  );
}

