## Exercise 2 — defaults that make the common case free

Edit `ex2/solution.tsx`. `ResultList` renders a list, but callers should not
have to think about the empty state unless they want to override it.

Export:

```tsx
export function ResultList(props: {
  items: { id: string; label: string }[];
  empty?: ReactNode;
  renderItem?: (item: { id: string; label: string }) => ReactNode;
}): ReactElement;
```

Rules:

- with items, render a `<ul>` with one `<li key={item.id}>` per item; the
  content of each `<li>` is `renderItem(item)` when supplied, otherwise
  `item.label`;
- with no items, render the `empty` slot; when `empty` was not supplied, render
  the default `<p>No results</p>`;
- an `empty` of `null` means the caller explicitly wants nothing rendered —
  honour that, do not fall back to the default.

So `empty={null}` renders nothing at all, and omitting `empty` renders
`No results`. That distinction is the exercise.

```
uv run dojo test react v3rc01_component_api_design ex2
```

references.solution.tsx:
// v3rc01_component_api_design / ex2 - reference solution.
import type { ReactElement, ReactNode } from "react";

type Item = { id: string; label: string };

export function ResultList({
  items,
  empty,
  renderItem = (item: Item) => item.label,
}: {
  items: Item[];
  empty?: ReactNode;
  renderItem?: (item: Item) => ReactNode;
}): ReactElement {
  if (items.length === 0) {
    // `empty === undefined` is the only test that separates "the caller said
    // nothing" from "the caller explicitly asked for nothing". `??` and `||`
    // both collapse those two cases together.
    return <>{empty === undefined ? <p>No results</p> : empty}</>;
  }

  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map((item) => (
        <li key={item.id}>{renderItem(item)}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}

