## Challenge — the composed select

Edit `challenge/solution.tsx`. Build a `Select` that is compound,
hybrid-controlled and polymorphic at once.

Export:

```tsx
export function Select(props: {
  value?: string;
  defaultValue?: string;
  onChange?: (next: string) => void;
  children: ReactNode;
}): ReactElement;

export function SelectTrigger<T extends ElementType = "button">(
  props: { as?: T; placeholder?: string } &
    Omit<ComponentPropsWithoutRef<T>, "as" | "placeholder" | "children">
): ReactElement;

export function SelectOptions(props: { children: ReactNode }): ReactElement;

export function SelectOption(props: {
  value: string;
  children: ReactNode;
}): ReactElement;
```

Behaviour:

- `Select` owns the open/closed state always, and owns the selected value only
  when uncontrolled — the mode is frozen on the first render, as in ex5;
- `SelectTrigger` renders `as` (default `button`, always with
  `type="button"` when it renders a button), showing the label of the selected
  option, or `placeholder` when nothing is selected. It carries
  `aria-haspopup="listbox"` and `aria-expanded`. Clicking toggles the list, and
  a caller-supplied `onClick` still runs;
- `SelectOptions` renders `<ul role="listbox">` only while open;
- `SelectOption` renders `<li role="option">` with `aria-selected`, and a click
  selects the value, calls `onChange`, and closes the list;
- the trigger has to display the *label* of the selected option, which lives
  inside a `SelectOption` somewhere below it. Solve that without walking
  `children` and without `cloneElement`.

Honest difficulty note: nothing here is a new idea after ex1–ex8. The work is
the last bullet — a child needs to publish something upward — and keeping the
three patterns from leaking into each other.

