## Exercise 5 — a fallback with a retry

Edit `ex5/solution.tsx`. Make the fallback a function so callers can build a
real recovery UI.

Export:

```tsx
export class RetryBoundary extends Component<
  {
    children: ReactNode;
    fallback: (args: { error: Error; reset: () => void }) => ReactNode;
  },
  { error: Error | null; attempt: number }
> {}
```

Behaviour:

- on catching, render `fallback({ error, reset })`;
- `reset` clears the error **and** increments `attempt`;
- the children are rendered inside a wrapper keyed on `attempt`, so a reset
  genuinely remounts the subtree rather than re-rendering the same instances;
- `reset` must have a stable identity across renders — a caller may pass it
  straight to a memoised button.

The gate renders a child that throws on its first mount and succeeds on its
second, clicks the retry button in the fallback, and asserts the child's content
appears. It also asserts the child's `useEffect` ran twice, proving a remount
rather than a re-render.

