## Exercise 7 — announcing without interrupting

Edit `ex7/solution.tsx`. Tell a screen reader user that something finished,
without moving their focus or cutting them off.

Export:

```tsx
export function SearchResults(props: {
  query: string;
  status: "idle" | "loading" | "done" | "error";
  count: number;
}): ReactElement;
```

Requirements:

- a live region element is **always** in the DOM, even when there is nothing to
  say — it starts empty;
- when `status` becomes `"done"`, the region's text becomes
  `<count> results for <query>` — for `count: 3, query: "socks"`, that is
  `3 results for socks`;
- when `status` becomes `"error"`, the region's text becomes `Search failed`
  and the region is assertive for that message only;
- for everything else the region is polite;
- focus must never move — the gate focuses the search input before changing
  status and asserts it is still focused afterwards;
- the visible results list is separate from the live region; the region itself
  is visually hidden but must remain in the accessibility tree.

The gate mounts with `status: "idle"`, asserts an empty `status`-role element
exists, then re-renders with `"done"` and asserts the text and that focus did
not move.

```
uv run dojo test react v3rc03_accessibility_focus ex7
```

references.solution.tsx:
// v3rc03_accessibility_focus / ex7 - reference solution.
import type { CSSProperties, ReactElement } from "react";

// Visually hidden but still in the accessibility tree. display:none and
// visibility:hidden would remove it from the tree and announce nothing.
const srOnly: CSSProperties = {
  position: "absolute",
  width: 1,
  height: 1,
  margin: -1,
  padding: 0,
  overflow: "hidden",
  clipPath: "inset(50%)",
  whiteSpace: "nowrap",
};

export function SearchResults({
  query,
  status,
  count,
}: {
  query: string;
  status: "idle" | "loading" | "done" | "error";
  count: number;
}): ReactElement {
  const politeMessage = status === "done" ? `${count} results for ${query}` : "";

  return (
    <div>
      {/* Both regions are always mounted and start empty: a live region
          announces mutations to its contents, so one that arrives with its
          text already inside is frequently not announced at all. */}
      <div style={srOnly} role="status">
        {politeMessage}
      </div>
      {/* Separate node for the assertive case. Flipping aria-live on a live
          region while it is live is not reliably implemented. */}
      <div style={srOnly} role="alert">
        {status === "error" ? "Search failed" : ""}
      </div>

      {status === "loading" && <p>Searching…</p>}
      {status === "done" && <p>{count} results</p>}
      {status === "error" && <p>Something went wrong. Try again.</p>}
    </div>
  );
}

