## Challenge — the menu button

Edit `challenge/solution.tsx`. Implement the WAI-ARIA menu button contract in
full.

Export:

```tsx
export function MenuButton(props: {
  label: string;
  items: { id: string; label: string; onSelect: () => void; disabled?: boolean }[];
}): ReactElement;
```

The contract, all of which the gate exercises:

- the trigger is a `<button type="button">` with `aria-haspopup="menu"` and
  `aria-expanded`;
- the menu is a `<ul role="menu">` labelled by the trigger, with
  `<li role="menuitem">` children, rendered only while open;
- **Enter** or **Space** or **ArrowDown** on the trigger opens the menu and
  focuses the **first** enabled item; **ArrowUp** opens it and focuses the
  **last** enabled item;
- inside the menu: ArrowDown/ArrowUp move between enabled items and **wrap**;
  Home and End jump to first and last enabled; Escape closes and returns focus
  to the trigger; Tab closes the menu and lets focus move on normally;
- Enter or Space on an item calls its `onSelect`, closes the menu and returns
  focus to the trigger;
- disabled items are `aria-disabled="true"`, are skipped by all keyboard
  movement, and do nothing when activated — they are **not** `disabled`, so they
  remain discoverable;
- the menu is a single tab stop implemented with roving tabindex.

Honest difficulty note: every piece appeared in ex1–ex8. The work is the state
machine — "open with first focused", "open with last focused" and "closed" are
three states, and expressing them as two booleans is where this gets away from
people.

```
uv run dojo test react v3rc03_accessibility_focus challenge
```

references.solution.tsx:
// v3rc03_accessibility_focus / challenge - reference solution.
import { useEffect, useId, useReducer, useRef } from "react";
import type { KeyboardEvent, ReactElement } from "react";

type MenuItem = {
  id: string;
  label: string;
  onSelect: () => void;
  disabled?: boolean;
};

type State = { open: boolean; active: number };
type Action =
  | { type: "OPEN_FIRST" }
  | { type: "OPEN_LAST" }
  | { type: "MOVE"; delta: 1 | -1 }
  | { type: "EDGE"; edge: "first" | "last" }
  | { type: "CLOSE" };

function makeReducer(enabled: number[]) {
  return function reduce(state: State, action: Action): State {
    if (enabled.length === 0) return { open: false, active: -1 };
    const first = enabled[0];
    const last = enabled[enabled.length - 1];

    switch (action.type) {
      case "OPEN_FIRST":
        return { open: true, active: first };
      case "OPEN_LAST":
        return { open: true, active: last };
      case "EDGE":
        return { open: true, active: action.edge === "first" ? first : last };
      case "MOVE": {
        // Movement happens in "enabled" space, so skipping disabled items is
        // handled once instead of in five separate places.
        const position = enabled.indexOf(state.active);
        const next = (position + action.delta + enabled.length) % enabled.length;
        return { open: true, active: enabled[next] };
      }
      case "CLOSE":
        return { open: false, active: -1 };
    }
  };
}

export function MenuButton({
  label,
  items,
}: {
  label: string;
  items: MenuItem[];
}): ReactElement {
  const enabled = items.flatMap((item, index) => (item.disabled ? [] : [index]));
  const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(makeReducer(enabled), {
    open: false,
    active: -1,
  });

  const baseId = useId();
  const triggerId = `${baseId}-trigger`;
  const menuId = `${baseId}-menu`;
  const triggerRef = useRef<HTMLButtonElement>(null);
  const itemRefs = useRef<(HTMLLIElement | null)[]>([]);

  // Focus moves in an effect, not in the handler: on the render that opens the
  // menu the items did not exist yet when the key was pressed.
  useEffect(() => {
    if (!state.open || state.active < 0) return;
    itemRefs.current[state.active]?.focus();
  }, [state.open, state.active]);

  function closeAndReturnFocus() {
    dispatch({ type: "CLOSE" });
    triggerRef.current?.focus();
  }

  function onTriggerKeyDown(event: KeyboardEvent<HTMLButtonElement>) {
    if (event.key === "ArrowDown" || event.key === "Enter" || event.key === " ") {
      event.preventDefault();
      dispatch({ type: "OPEN_FIRST" });
    } else if (event.key === "ArrowUp") {
      event.preventDefault();
      dispatch({ type: "OPEN_LAST" });
    }
  }

  function onMenuKeyDown(event: KeyboardEvent<HTMLUListElement>) {
    switch (event.key) {
      case "ArrowDown":
        event.preventDefault();
        return dispatch({ type: "MOVE", delta: 1 });
      case "ArrowUp":
        event.preventDefault();
        return dispatch({ type: "MOVE", delta: -1 });
      case "Home":
        event.preventDefault();
        return dispatch({ type: "EDGE", edge: "first" });
      case "End":
        event.preventDefault();
        return dispatch({ type: "EDGE", edge: "last" });
      case "Escape":
        event.preventDefault();
        return closeAndReturnFocus();
      case "Tab":
        // Close but do NOT take focus back, or the user is trapped.
        return dispatch({ type: "CLOSE" });
      case "Enter":
      case " ": {
        event.preventDefault();
        const item = items[state.active];
        if (item && !item.disabled) {
          item.onSelect();
          closeAndReturnFocus();
        }
        return;
      }
      default:
        return;
    }
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <button
        ref={triggerRef}
        id={triggerId}
        type="button"
        aria-haspopup="menu"
        aria-expanded={state.open}
        aria-controls={state.open ? menuId : undefined}
        onClick={() => dispatch(state.open ? { type: "CLOSE" } : { type: "OPEN_FIRST" })}
        onKeyDown={onTriggerKeyDown}
      >
        {label}
      </button>

      {state.open && (
        <ul
          id={menuId}
          role="menu"
          aria-labelledby={triggerId}
          onKeyDown={onMenuKeyDown}
        >
          {items.map((item, index) => (
            <li
              key={item.id}
              role="menuitem"
              ref={(node) => {
                itemRefs.current[index] = node;
              }}
              // Roving tabindex: the menu is one tab stop.
              tabIndex={index === state.active ? 0 : -1}
              // aria-disabled, not disabled: the item stays discoverable and is
              // announced as unavailable rather than being invisible.
              aria-disabled={item.disabled ? true : undefined}
              onClick={() => {
                if (item.disabled) return;
                item.onSelect();
                closeAndReturnFocus();
              }}
            >
              {item.label}
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}

// v3rc03_accessibility_focus / review1 - reference solution.
import { useId } from "react";
import type { CSSProperties, ReactElement } from "react";

export const DEFECTS: string[] = [
  "aria-hidden=\"true\" on a wrapper containing a focusable button: a tab stop that is absent from the accessibility tree",
  "disabled on Apply removed it from the tab order, so keyboard users could not find it or learn why it was unavailable - aria-disabled is correct here",
  "the search input had a placeholder and no label, so it had no accessible name",
  "span role=\"button\" tabIndex={3}: a positive tabIndex reorders the whole document, and the element needed no ARIA at all as a real button",
  "the results count changed with nothing announcing it - it needs a live region that is mounted and empty before the message arrives",
];

const srOnly: CSSProperties = {
  position: "absolute",
  width: 1,
  height: 1,
  margin: -1,
  padding: 0,
  overflow: "hidden",
  clipPath: "inset(50%)",
  whiteSpace: "nowrap",
};

export function FilterPanel({
  query,
  onQueryChange,
  onApply,
  onClear,
  count,
}: {
  query: string;
  onQueryChange: (next: string) => void;
  onApply: () => void;
  onClear: () => void;
  count: number;
}): ReactElement {
  const searchId = useId();
  const applyBlocked = query.trim() === "";

  return (
    <div>
      {/* Fault 1: the aria-hidden wrapper is gone. Nothing focusable may sit
          inside a subtree hidden from assistive technology. */}
      <div className="filter-row">
        {/* Fault 3: a real label. The placeholder stays as a hint rather than
            standing in for the name. */}
        <label htmlFor={searchId}>Search</label>
        <input
          id={searchId}
          type="search"
          placeholder="Search filters…"
          value={query}
          onChange={(event) => onQueryChange(event.target.value)}
        />

        {/* Fault 4: a real button, no role, no tabIndex. A positive tabIndex
            anywhere reorders the entire document's tab sequence. */}
        <button type="button" onClick={onClear}>
          Clear
        </button>
      </div>

      {/* Fault 2: aria-disabled keeps it reachable and announces it as
          unavailable - but it does not stop the click, so the handler must. */}
      <button
        type="button"
        aria-disabled={applyBlocked || undefined}
        aria-describedby={applyBlocked ? `${searchId}-why` : undefined}
        onClick={() => {
          if (applyBlocked) return;
          onApply();
        }}
      >
        Apply
      </button>
      {applyBlocked && (
        <p id={`${searchId}-why`}>Enter a search term before applying.</p>
      )}

      {/* Fault 5: always mounted so a change is a mutation the screen reader
          notices, polite so it never interrupts, and focus never moves. */}
      <p style={srOnly} role="status">
        {count} results
      </p>
      <p aria-hidden="true">{count} results</p>
    </div>
  );
}

lessons.fullReadme:
# V3·RC4 — Animation and layout without jank

## Why this sector exists

Animation is where a UI stops feeling like a document and starts feeling like an
object. It is also where a competent React application most often becomes
visibly bad on a mid-range Android phone, because the same code that runs at
60fps on a development laptop runs at 22fps on hardware with a quarter of the
single-core performance and a thermally throttled GPU.

The knowledge that separates the two outcomes is small and specific: which
properties are cheap, when the browser is forced to do work synchronously, and
how to hand an animation to the compositor and get out of the way.

## Concepts

### The pipeline, per property

| you change | style | layout | paint | composite |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `width`, `top`, `margin`, `font-size` | yes | yes | yes | yes |
| `color`, `background-color`, `box-shadow` | yes | no | yes | yes |
| `transform`, `opacity` (promoted layer) | yes | no | no | yes |

Anything in the third row can be animated by the compositor without the main
thread. That is the target.

### Layout thrash, and the fix

```ts
// Bad: n forced layouts.
for (const row of rows) {
  row.style.height = `${row.offsetHeight * 2}px`;
}

// Good: one layout.
const heights = rows.map((row) => row.offsetHeight);   // all reads
rows.forEach((row, i) => {                             // all writes
  row.style.height = `${heights[i] * 2}px`;
});
```

The rule generalises past this loop: any time you find a read of a geometry
property inside a loop that also writes, you have this bug.

### FLIP, in full

```tsx
function useFlip(keys: string[], refs: Map<string, HTMLElement>) {
  const previous = useRef(new Map<string, DOMRect>());

  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    for (const [key, node] of refs) {
      const before = previous.current.get(key);
      const after = node.getBoundingClientRect();      // LAST
      if (before) {
        const dx = before.left - after.left;           // INVERT
        const dy = before.top - after.top;
        if (dx || dy) {
          node.style.transition = "none";
          node.style.transform = `translate(${dx}px, ${dy}px)`;
          requestAnimationFrame(() => {                // PLAY
            node.style.transition = "transform 200ms";
            node.style.transform = "";
          });
        }
      }
      previous.current.set(key, after);                // FIRST, for next time
    }
  }, [keys]);
}
```

The `requestAnimationFrame` between the invert and the play is essential.
Without it the browser coalesces both style changes and never renders the
inverted state, so nothing animates.

### Exit animation

```tsx
type Phase = "enter" | "shown" | "exit";

function useExitTransition(show: boolean, durationMs: number) {
  const [phase, setPhase] = useState<Phase | null>(show ? "shown" : null);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (show) { setPhase("shown"); return; }
    if (phase === null) return;
    setPhase("exit");
    const id = setTimeout(() => setPhase(null), durationMs);
    return () => clearTimeout(id);
  }, [show]);

  return phase;
}
```

The timeout is the primary mechanism, not a fallback: `transitionend` is
unreliable enough that building on it alone produces elements that never
disappear.

### Reduced motion as a subscription

```tsx
const QUERY = "(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)";

function subscribe(cb: () => void) {
  const mql = window.matchMedia(QUERY);
  mql.addEventListener("change", cb);
  return () => mql.removeEventListener("change", cb);
}

export function usePrefersReducedMotion() {
  return useSyncExternalStore(
    subscribe,
    () => window.matchMedia(QUERY).matches,
    () => false                              // server snapshot
  );
}
```

## Common mistakes

- **Animating `left`/`top`/`width`/`height`.** Layout every frame.
- **Reading geometry inside a write loop.** *n* forced layouts.
- **`useEffect` for measure-then-position.** One painted frame of the wrong
  position.
- **`will-change` left on permanently.** GPU memory, on every instance.
- **An uncancelled `requestAnimationFrame`.** Keeps running after unmount,
  holding the component's whole closure alive.
- **Relying on `transitionend` alone.** Elements that never unmount.
- **`transitionend` from a child.** It bubbles; check `event.target`.
- **Reading `matchMedia` once.** Misses the user changing the setting.
- **`ResizeObserver` that resizes what it observes.** Infinite loop.
- **Animating a hundred items individually in JavaScript** when one CSS
  transition on a parent would do.

## The interview angle

"Why is `transform` faster than `left`?" is the question, and the strong answer
names the pipeline and the thread: `left` invalidates layout, which must be
recomputed on the main thread and then repainted, whereas `transform` on a
promoted layer is handled by the compositor, which can run while the main thread
is busy. Follow that with layout thrashing as the second-largest cause of jank,
and with the read-then-write fix, and you have covered most of what a front-end
performance interview wants here.

## Check yourself

1. Name the five stages of the rendering pipeline in order.
2. Which two properties can be animated without layout or paint, and what makes
   that possible?
3. What is a forced synchronous layout, and name four properties whose *read*
   causes one.
4. Write the two-pass fix for a loop that measures and styles *n* elements.
5. When must you use `useLayoutEffect` instead of `useEffect`, and what does it
   cost?
6. Explain FLIP in four steps.
7. Why does FLIP need a `requestAnimationFrame` between invert and play?
8. Give three reasons `transitionend` might never fire.
9. What is `will-change` for, and what is the cost of leaving it on?
10. Why is a `ResizeObserver` better than a `resize` listener for measuring a
    component?
11. What does `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` ask you to do — and what does it
    not ask you to do?
12. Why is `useSyncExternalStore` the right hook for a media query?
13. Name three ways to animate an element to `height: auto` and a failure mode
    for each.
14. What is the frame budget at 60Hz, and how much of it is realistically
    yours?

