## Exercise 2 — measure before paint

Edit `ex2/solution.tsx`. A tooltip that flips to the other side when it would
overflow its container, with no visible flash.

Export:

```tsx
export function Tooltip(props: {
  text: string;
  anchorLeft: number;
  containerWidth: number;
  onPlaced?: (side: "right" | "left") => void;
}): ReactElement;
```

Requirements:

- the tooltip renders at `anchorLeft` on the right side by default;
- after it exists, measure its own width with `getBoundingClientRect()`; if
  `anchorLeft + width > containerWidth`, move it to the left side, positioned at
  `anchorLeft - width`;
- the corrected position must be applied **before the browser paints** — the
  user must never see the tooltip on the wrong side;
- call `onPlaced` once per placement decision with the side chosen;
- the element carries `data-testid="tooltip"` and `data-side` with the chosen
  side.

The gate patches `requestAnimationFrame` to detect a paint between the initial
render and the correction, and fails if one occurred. It also checks the
placement decision for a tooltip that fits and one that does not.

```
uv run dojo test react v3rc04_animation_layout ex2
```

exercises.hints:
## Hint 1

This is a genuine chicken-and-egg: you cannot know the width until it is
rendered, and you cannot place it correctly until you know the width. So it
renders twice — once provisionally, once corrected — and the whole exercise is
making the first of those two invisible.

## Hint 2

`useEffect` runs *after* the browser paints. So the sequence is: commit the
wrong position, paint it, run the effect, commit the right position, paint
again. That is one visible frame of the tooltip in the wrong place, and it is
the flicker everybody has seen in a badly built popover.

`useLayoutEffect` runs synchronously after commit and *before* paint, so both
commits happen within one frame and the user only ever sees the corrected one.

## Hint 3

The shape:

```tsx
const ref = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);
const [side, setSide] = useState<"right" | "left">("right");

useLayoutEffect(() => {
  const width = ref.current?.getBoundingClientRect().width ?? 0;
  const next = anchorLeft + width > containerWidth ? "left" : "right";
  setSide(next);
  onPlaced?.(next);
}, [anchorLeft, containerWidth, text]);
```

Setting state from a layout effect is legal and is exactly what this hook is
for — React re-renders synchronously before yielding to the browser.

references.solution.tsx:
// v3rc04_animation_layout / ex2 - reference solution.
import { useLayoutEffect, useRef, useState } from "react";
import type { ReactElement } from "react";

export function Tooltip({
  text,
  anchorLeft,
  containerWidth,
  onPlaced,
}: {
  text: string;
  anchorLeft: number;
  containerWidth: number;
  onPlaced?: (side: "right" | "left") => void;
}): ReactElement {
  const ref = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);
  const [side, setSide] = useState<"right" | "left">("right");
  const [width, setWidth] = useState(0);

  // useLayoutEffect, not useEffect: this runs synchronously after commit and
  // before the browser paints, so the provisional position is never displayed.
  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    const measured = ref.current?.getBoundingClientRect().width ?? 0;
    const next: "right" | "left" =
      anchorLeft + measured > containerWidth ? "left" : "right";
    setWidth(measured);
    setSide(next);
    onPlaced?.(next);
    // onPlaced deliberately omitted: callers pass it inline and it would
    // re-run the measurement on every parent render.
    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps
  }, [anchorLeft, containerWidth, text]);

  const left = side === "right" ? anchorLeft : Math.max(anchorLeft - width, 0);

  return (
    <div
      ref={ref}
      data-testid="tooltip"
      data-side={side}
      style={{ position: "absolute", left, whiteSpace: "nowrap" }}
    >
      {text}
    </div>
  );
}

