## Exercise 5 — FLIP

Edit `ex5/solution.tsx`. Animate a reordering list from its old positions to its
new ones.

Export:

```tsx
export function FlipList(props: {
  items: { id: string; label: string }[];
  durationMs?: number;
}): ReactElement;
```

Requirements:

- one `<li data-id={id}>` per item, keyed by id;
- when `items` is reordered, each item whose position changed animates from
  where it used to be to where it now is;
- the animation must be a `transform` transition — no `top`, `left` or
  positioning changes;
- items that did not move must not be given a transition at all;
- a new item appearing must not animate from nowhere; it simply appears;
- the transition duration is `durationMs`, default `200`.

The gate reorders the list and, between the commit and the next animation
frame, reads each moved item's inline `transform` and asserts it equals the
inverse of the item's displacement — i.e. that the invert step happened.

```
uv run dojo test react v3rc04_animation_layout ex5
```

exercises.hints:
## Hint 1

The name is the algorithm. **F**irst: record each item's box before the change.
**L**ast: let the change happen and record the new box. **I**nvert: apply a
transform that puts each item visually back where it was. **P**lay: remove the
transform with a transition, and the browser animates from the inverted position
to the real one.

The reason this is cheap: the layout happens once, when React commits. The
animation is a transform, so it is pure compositing.

## Hint 2

The "First" measurement is the tricky one to place. You want the boxes from
*before* React re-rendered — so record them in a ref at the end of every layout
effect, ready for next time:

```tsx
const previous = useRef(new Map<string, DOMRect>());
useLayoutEffect(() => {
  for (const [id, node] of nodes) {
    const after = node.getBoundingClientRect();
    const before = previous.current.get(id);
    // …invert if before exists and differs…
    previous.current.set(id, after);
  }
});
```

An item with no `before` entry is new, which is how "do not animate new items"
falls out for free.

## Hint 3

The play step needs a frame between setting the transform and clearing it:

```tsx
node.style.transition = "none";
node.style.transform = `translate(${dx}px, ${dy}px)`;
requestAnimationFrame(() => {
  node.style.transition = `transform ${durationMs}ms`;
  node.style.transform = "";
});
```

Without the `requestAnimationFrame` the browser coalesces both writes, never
observes the inverted state, and nothing animates. Forcing a read
(`node.offsetHeight`) between them works too and is the older idiom.

references.solution.tsx:
// v3rc04_animation_layout / ex5 - reference solution.
import { useLayoutEffect, useRef } from "react";
import type { ReactElement } from "react";

export function FlipList({
  items,
  durationMs = 200,
}: {
  items: { id: string; label: string }[];
  durationMs?: number;
}): ReactElement {
  const nodes = useRef(new Map<string, HTMLLIElement>());
  const previous = useRef(new Map<string, DOMRect>());

  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    // LAST - one read pass, so one forced layout for the whole list.
    const current = new Map<string, DOMRect>();
    for (const [id, node] of nodes.current) {
      current.set(id, node.getBoundingClientRect());
    }

    // INVERT - one write pass.
    const moved: HTMLLIElement[] = [];
    for (const [id, node] of nodes.current) {
      const before = previous.current.get(id);
      const after = current.get(id);
      // No "before" means the item is new: it appears, it does not fly in.
      if (!before || !after) continue;

      const dx = before.left - after.left;
      const dy = before.top - after.top;
      if (dx === 0 && dy === 0) continue;

      node.style.transition = "none";
      node.style.transform = `translate(${dx}px, ${dy}px)`;
      moved.push(node);
    }

    // PLAY - a frame later, or the browser coalesces both writes and never
    // observes the inverted state, so nothing animates.
    if (moved.length > 0) {
      requestAnimationFrame(() => {
        for (const node of moved) {
          node.style.transition = `transform ${durationMs}ms ease-out`;
          node.style.transform = "";
        }
      });
    }

    // FIRST, for the next change.
    previous.current = current;
  }, [items, durationMs]);

  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map((item) => (
        <li
          key={item.id}
          data-id={item.id}
          ref={(node) => {
            if (node) nodes.current.set(item.id, node);
            else nodes.current.delete(item.id);
          }}
        >
          {item.label}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}

