# V3·RC5 — Routing patterns and the URL as state

## Why this sector exists

Every application has a set of state that determines what is on screen. In a
server-rendered application that set is largely in the URL, because the URL is
how the request was made. In a single-page application it is easy for that set
to migrate entirely into memory, at which point the application stops behaving
like a website: links do not work, the back button does something surprising,
reload loses the user's place, and nothing can be shared.

Treating the URL as state is how you get those properties back, and it is mostly
a discipline rather than a technology.

## Concepts

### Reading the location as a subscription

```tsx
const listeners = new Set<() => void>();

function emit() {
  for (const listener of listeners) listener();
}

let patched = false;
function patchHistory() {
  if (patched) return;
  patched = true;
  for (const method of ["pushState", "replaceState"] as const) {
    const original = history[method].bind(history);
    history[method] = (...args: Parameters<typeof original>) => {
      original(...args);
      emit();                      // pushState fires no event of its own
    };
  }
}

export function subscribe(listener: () => void) {
  patchHistory();
  listeners.add(listener);
  window.addEventListener("popstate", emit);
  return () => {
    listeners.delete(listener);
    window.removeEventListener("popstate", emit);
  };
}

export function useLocationHref() {
  return useSyncExternalStore(
    subscribe,
    () => window.location.href,     // a string: Object.is-stable
    () => "http://localhost/"       // server snapshot
  );
}
```

Returning the href string rather than a parsed object is the detail that keeps
this from looping: `new URL(...)` would be a fresh object on every snapshot
call, and React would conclude the store changed every time.

### Writing: push or replace

```tsx
function setParam(key: string, value: string | null, mode: "push" | "replace") {
  const url = new URL(window.location.href);
  if (value === null || value === "") url.searchParams.delete(key);
  else url.searchParams.set(key, value);

  const method = mode === "push" ? "pushState" : "replaceState";
  history[method](history.state, "", url);
}
```

Deleting on empty rather than setting an empty value keeps `?q=` out of the URL.
`?q=` parses as the empty string, which is not the same as absent, and the
difference shows up as a filter that is "active but matching everything".

### Matching a path

```tsx
export function matchPath(
  pattern: string,
  pathname: string
): Record<string, string> | null {
  const p = pattern.replace(/\/+$/, "").split("/");
  const a = pathname.replace(/\/+$/, "").split("/");
  if (p.length !== a.length) return null;

  const params: Record<string, string> = {};
  for (let i = 0; i < p.length; i++) {
    if (p[i].startsWith(":")) {
      if (a[i] === "") return null;
      params[p[i].slice(1)] = decodeURIComponent(a[i]);
    } else if (p[i] !== a[i]) {
      return null;
    }
  }
  return params;
}
```

### A guard that does not flash

```tsx
function Protected({ user, children }: ProtectedProps) {
  const allowed = user !== null;

  useEffect(() => {
    if (!allowed) history.replaceState(null, "", "/login");
  }, [allowed]);

  if (!allowed) return <Navigating to="/login" />;   // decided during render
  return children;
}
```

The `return` during render is the load-bearing line. Doing only the effect
renders and paints the protected content first.

## Common mistakes

- **Mirroring a param into `useState`.** Two sources of truth; drifts on Back.
- **Building a query string by hand.** Unencoded `&` and `+`.
- **`set` when you meant `append`.** Silently collapses a multi-select to one.
- **Leaving `?q=` in the URL.** Empty string is not absent.
- **Pushing on every keystroke.** Forty history entries.
- **Subscribing only to `popstate`.** Misses your own navigations.
- **`getSnapshot` returning a new object.** Infinite render loop.
- **Redirecting in an effect only.** One painted frame of protected content.
- **Creating the `lazy()` component inside render.** A new import every render.
- **Restoring scroll by pathname.** The same path can have two positions.
- **Forgetting the server rewrite.** Deep links 404 on refresh in production
  unless the server serves `index.html` for unknown paths.

## The interview angle

"What state would you put in the URL, and why?" is the question. The answer that
lands names the properties the URL has that memory does not — shareable,
bookmarkable, reloadable, back-button-aware, indexable — and then gives a rule:
anything that changes what the user is looking at goes in the URL; anything
in-progress or private does not.

A strong follow-up is push versus replace, framed as "what should the back
button do", and the drift bug, framed as "the URL is the state, so read it
rather than copying it".

## Check yourself

1. Name five properties the URL has that `useState` does not.
2. What is the difference between `set` and `append` on `URLSearchParams`, and
   when does it matter?
3. Why does `?q=` behave differently from omitting `q`?
4. When should a navigation push, and when should it replace?
5. Why does subscribing to `popstate` alone miss navigations?
6. Why must `getSnapshot` be `Object.is`-stable, and what goes wrong if it is
   not?
7. What is wrong with `useState(params.get("q") ?? "")`?
8. Why is a redirect performed only in an effect a real bug rather than a
   cosmetic one?
9. What must a server do for deep links to survive a refresh?
10. Why should a layout route not unmount when navigating between its children,
    and what makes that happen?
11. Where would you store a per-entry scroll position, and why not keyed by
    path?
12. What must you do for focus after a client-side navigation, and why?
13. What does `history.scrollRestoration = "manual"` buy you?
14. Give two things that must never go in a URL.

