## Challenge — the deep-linkable list

Edit `challenge/solution.tsx`. Every piece of view state in the URL, and the
interactions between them handled correctly.

Export:

```tsx
export type ListState = {
  q: string;
  tags: string[];
  sort: "name" | "date";
  dir: "asc" | "desc";
  page: number;
};

export function parseListState(search: string): ListState;
export function serialiseListState(state: ListState): string;

export function InvoiceList(props: {
  invoices: { id: string; name: string; date: string; tags: string[] }[];
  pageSize?: number;
}): ReactElement;
```

Requirements:

- `parseListState` reads all five values with sensible defaults: empty query, no
  tags, `sort: "name"`, `dir: "asc"`, `page: 1`. It must be **total** — a
  garbage `?page=banana` or `?sort=colour` yields the default rather than
  throwing or producing an invalid state;
- `serialiseListState` omits every value that equals its default, so the clean
  URL for a default view is `""` and not `?q=&sort=name&dir=asc&page=1`;
- the rendered list applies query, tags, sort, direction and pagination, and
  shows `Page N of M`;
- changing the query, the tags, or the sort **resets the page to 1** — and does
  so in a **single history write**, so Back never lands on a state the user
  never saw;
- changing the sort key to the one already selected flips `dir`;
- typing in the search box replaces; every other control pushes;
- the whole component reads its state from the URL only. There is no `useState`
  for anything in `ListState`.

Honest difficulty note: no new API after ex1–ex8. The difficulty is entirely in
the last three bullets — the reset-plus-write must be one operation, and the
"omit defaults" rule has to survive round-tripping.

