## Review 1 — the drift bug

Edit `review1/solution.tsx`. The stub exports a `TeamPicker` that works
perfectly until you press Back:

```tsx
export function TeamPicker({ teams }: { teams: Team[] }) {
  const { search } = useLocation();
  const [selected, setSelected] = useState(
    new URLSearchParams(search).get("team") ?? ""
  );

  useEffect(() => {
    const url = new URL(window.location.href);
    url.searchParams.set("team", selected);
    history.pushState(null, "", url);
  }, [selected]);

  return (
    <select value={selected} onChange={(e) => setSelected(e.target.value)}>
      …
    </select>
  );
}
```

Three faults follow from the same root cause. Fix all of them:

1. pressing Back changes the URL but not the select, and the two disagree from
   then on;
2. the effect writes to the URL on **mount**, adding a spurious history entry
   before the user has done anything;
3. selecting the empty option leaves `?team=` in the URL rather than removing
   the parameter.

Requirements:

- the component must have no `useState` and no `useEffect` at all when you are
  done;
- selecting a team pushes one entry; Back restores the previous selection and
  the select reflects it;
- mounting writes nothing;
- the empty selection removes the parameter.

Also export `const ROOT_CAUSE: string` — one sentence naming the single mistake
the three faults all come from.

```
uv run dojo test react v3rc05_routing_url_state review1
```

references.solution.tsx:
// v3rc05_routing_url_state / review1 - reference solution.
import type { ReactElement } from "react";
import { useLocation } from "./location-store";

export const ROOT_CAUSE: string =
  "The component copied URL state into useState and kept the copy in sync with an effect, creating a second source of truth that only flows one way - so any change the browser makes, such as Back, is never reflected back into the copy.";

type Team = { id: string; name: string };

export function TeamPicker({ teams }: { teams: Team[] }): ReactElement {
  const { search } = useLocation();

  // Read during render from the one source of truth. No state to drift, no
  // effect to fire on mount, and Back updates this automatically because the
  // location store notifies on popstate.
  const selected = new URLSearchParams(search).get("team") ?? "";

  function select(next: string) {
    const url = new URL(window.location.href);
    // Empty means "no team", which is an absent parameter rather than an empty
    // one - `?team=` parses back as the empty string, not as null.
    if (next === "") url.searchParams.delete("team");
    else url.searchParams.set("team", next);
    // A discrete choice, so it pushes: Back should restore the previous team.
    history.pushState(history.state, "", url);
  }

  return (
    <select value={selected} onChange={(event) => select(event.target.value)}>
      <option value="">All teams</option>
      {teams.map((team) => (
        <option key={team.id} value={team.id}>
          {team.name}
        </option>
      ))}
    </select>
  );
}

