## Hint 1

The distinction is what the hook's *contract* is with. `useSelection` exists to
make a list behave in a certain way — its contract is with the UI, so testing it
through a UI tests the real thing. `useLocalStorage`'s contract is with the
caller and with a browser API; there is no rendering involved, and building a
component to observe it would only add noise.

## Hint 2

The host component is disposable and lives in the test file:

```tsx
function Host({ items }: { items: string[] }) {
  const { selected, toggle, clear } = useSelection(items);
  return (
    <>
      {items.map((item) => (
        <label key={item}>
          <input type="checkbox" checked={selected.includes(item)}
                 onChange={() => toggle(item)} />
          {item}
        </label>
      ))}
      <button type="button" onClick={clear}>Clear</button>
    </>
  );
}
```

## Hint 3

`renderHook` returns `{ result, rerender, unmount }` and `result.current` is the
latest return value. Two details: state updates must be wrapped in `act`, and
`rerender(newProps)` is how you test a hook's response to changing arguments —
which is the third assertion here and the one that catches a hook that
captured its key on the first render.

Remember to clear `localStorage` between tests, or the second test reads what
the first wrote.

