## Exercise 2 — why did this render

Edit `ex2/solution.tsx`. A render count tells you something happened. Build the
thing that tells you what.

Export:

```tsx
export type RenderReason =
  | { kind: "mount" }
  | { kind: "props"; changed: { key: string; from: unknown; to: unknown }[] }
  | { kind: "other" };

export function useRenderReason(
  props: Record<string, unknown>,
  onReason: (reason: RenderReason) => void
): void;
```

Requirements:

- on the first render, report `{ kind: "mount" }`;
- on subsequent renders, compare the current props object with the previous one
  **key by key** using `Object.is`, and report every key that differs — including
  keys added and keys removed;
- when props are identical but the component rendered anyway — state, context, or
  a parent re-render with no prop change — report `{ kind: "other" }`;
- report from an effect, not from the render body, and report exactly once per
  commit;
- the hook must not itself cause a render.

The gate renders a component with a stable `label` and an inline
`onSelect={() => {}}`, re-renders the parent three times without changing
anything visible, and asserts the reported reason each time is `props` with
`changed` containing exactly `onSelect` — the memoisation defeat that is
invisible without this tool.

```
uv run dojo test react v3rc07_profiling_perf ex2
```

references.solution.tsx:
// v3rc07_profiling_perf / ex2 - reference solution.
import { useEffect, useRef } from "react";

export type RenderReason =
  | { kind: "mount" }
  | { kind: "props"; changed: { key: string; from: unknown; to: unknown }[] }
  | { kind: "other" };

export function useRenderReason(
  props: Record<string, unknown>,
  onReason: (reason: RenderReason) => void
): void {
  // A ref, because the previous props must survive a render without causing
  // one. State here would loop.
  const previous = useRef<Record<string, unknown> | null>(null);

  // In an effect, so it runs once per commit rather than once per render
  // attempt - React may render a component and discard the work.
  useEffect(() => {
    const before = previous.current;
    previous.current = props;

    if (before === null) {
      onReason({ kind: "mount" });
      return;
    }

    // The union of both key sets, so an added or removed prop is reported too.
    const keys = new Set([...Object.keys(before), ...Object.keys(props)]);
    const changed = [...keys]
      // Object.is, not ===, so NaN does not report as changed every render.
      .filter((key) => !Object.is(before[key], props[key]))
      .map((key) => ({ key, from: before[key], to: props[key] }));

    // "other" carries the most information: identical props and a render
    // anyway means state, a consumed context, or a parent render that memo
    // would have stopped - none of which a prop-level fix addresses.
    onReason(changed.length > 0 ? { kind: "props", changed } : { kind: "other" });
  });
}

