## Exercise 5 — the waterfall

Edit `ex5/solution.tsx`. Three requests, one round trip's worth of latency.

The stub renders `<Account>` which fetches a user, then renders `<Orders>` which
fetches orders by user id, which renders `<Recommendations>` which fetches by
order ids. Each request takes 200ms; the page takes 600ms.

Export:

```tsx
export function loadAccountPage(
  accountId: string,
  api: {
    fetchUser: (id: string) => Promise<User>;
    fetchOrders: (userId: string) => Promise<Order[]>;
    fetchPopular: () => Promise<Product[]>;
  }
): Promise<{ user: User; orders: Order[]; popular: Product[] }>;

export function AccountPage(props: {
  accountId: string;
  api: AccountApi;
}): ReactElement;
```

Requirements:

- `fetchPopular` does not depend on anything, so it must start **immediately**,
  in parallel with `fetchUser`;
- `fetchOrders` genuinely depends on the user's id, so it must wait — but only
  for `fetchUser`, not for `fetchPopular`;
- the measured critical path must therefore be **two** sequential requests, not
  three;
- a failure in `fetchPopular` must not prevent the user and orders from
  rendering — the recommendations section degrades to nothing;
- a failure in `fetchUser` rejects the whole load;
- `AccountPage` renders a single `Suspense` boundary over the whole page, so
  there is one loading state rather than three staggered ones.

The gate instruments the api functions with timestamps and asserts
`fetchPopular` started within 5ms of `fetchUser`, and that the total elapsed
time is under 450ms with 200ms stubs.

```
uv run dojo test react v3rc07_profiling_perf ex5
```

references.solution.tsx:
// v3rc07_profiling_perf / ex5 - reference solution.
import { Suspense, use, useMemo } from "react";
import type { ReactElement } from "react";

type User = { id: string; name: string };
type Order = { id: string; total: number };
type Product = { id: string; title: string };

type AccountApi = {
  fetchUser: (id: string) => Promise<User>;
  fetchOrders: (userId: string) => Promise<Order[]>;
  fetchPopular: () => Promise<Product[]>;
};

export async function loadAccountPage(
  accountId: string,
  api: AccountApi
): Promise<{ user: User; orders: Order[]; popular: Product[] }> {
  // Both independent requests start now. Calling the function starts the work;
  // `await` only decides when you look at the result.
  const userPromise = api.fetchUser(accountId);
  // The catch is attached where the promise is created, not at the await - an
  // unhandled rejection in the gap is reported even though it would have been
  // handled a few lines later.
  const popularPromise = api.fetchPopular().catch((): Product[] => []);

  const user = await userPromise;
  // The one genuine dependency: orders need the user's id.
  const orders = await api.fetchOrders(user.id);

  // Resolved long ago; this await costs nothing.
  const popular = await popularPromise;

  return { user, orders, popular };
}

function AccountBody({
  promise,
}: {
  promise: Promise<{ user: User; orders: Order[]; popular: Product[] }>;
}): ReactElement {
  const { user, orders, popular } = use(promise);
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>{user.name}</h1>
      <ul>
        {orders.map((order) => (
          <li key={order.id}>{order.total}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      {/* Degrades quietly: an empty array is what the catch above produced. */}
      {popular.length > 0 && (
        <ul>
          {popular.map((product) => (
            <li key={product.id}>{product.title}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}

export function AccountPage({
  accountId,
  api,
}: {
  accountId: string;
  api: AccountApi;
}): ReactElement {
  const promise = useMemo(() => loadAccountPage(accountId, api), [accountId, api]);

  // One boundary for the whole page rather than three nested ones, so the user
  // sees one loading state instead of a staircase of them.
  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<p role="status">Loading account</p>}>
      <AccountBody promise={promise} />
    </Suspense>
  );
}

