## Exercise 8 — windowing

Edit `ex8/solution.tsx`. Ten thousand rows, a bounded DOM.

Export:

```tsx
export function VirtualList<T>(props: {
  items: T[];
  rowHeight: number;
  height: number;
  overscan?: number;
  renderRow: (item: T, index: number) => ReactNode;
}): ReactElement;
```

Requirements:

- an outer scrollable `<div>` of the given `height` with `overflow: auto`,
  containing an inner spacer of height `items.length * rowHeight` so the
  scrollbar is proportionate;
- only the rows intersecting the viewport, plus `overscan` (default `3`) rows on
  each side, are rendered;
- each rendered row is absolutely positioned at `top: index * rowHeight` with
  the given height, and carries `data-index`;
- scrolling updates the window; the scroll handler must not do a layout read per
  event;
- the list has `role="list"` and rows `role="listitem"` with `aria-setsize` set
  to the full count and `aria-posinset` to the real index — so a screen reader
  says "item 12 of 10,000" rather than "of 20";
- an empty `items` array renders the container and nothing else, without
  throwing.

The gate renders 10,000 items at `rowHeight: 20` in a `height: 400` viewport and
asserts the committed DOM contains at most 30 row elements, that scrolling to
`scrollTop: 2000` renders rows around index 100, and that `aria-setsize` is
`10000`.

```
uv run dojo test react v3rc07_profiling_perf ex8
```

references.solution.tsx:
// v3rc07_profiling_perf / ex8 - reference solution.
import { useState } from "react";
import type { ReactElement, ReactNode, UIEvent } from "react";

export function VirtualList<T>({
  items,
  rowHeight,
  height,
  overscan = 3,
  renderRow,
}: {
  items: T[];
  rowHeight: number;
  height: number;
  overscan?: number;
  renderRow: (item: T, index: number) => ReactNode;
}): ReactElement {
  const [scrollTop, setScrollTop] = useState(0);

  const count = items.length;
  const first = Math.max(0, Math.floor(scrollTop / rowHeight) - overscan);
  const last = Math.min(count, Math.ceil((scrollTop + height) / rowHeight) + overscan);
  const window = items.slice(first, last);

  function handleScroll(event: UIEvent<HTMLDivElement>) {
    // Read from the event's currentTarget, not from a ref: the browser has
    // already computed this value, whereas a ref read is a forced layout on
    // every scroll event.
    setScrollTop(event.currentTarget.scrollTop);
  }

  return (
    <div
      role="list"
      onScroll={handleScroll}
      style={{ height, overflow: "auto" }}
    >
      {/* The spacer makes the scrollbar proportionate to the whole list rather
          than to the twenty rows that happen to be rendered. */}
      <div style={{ height: count * rowHeight, position: "relative" }}>
        {window.map((item, offset) => {
          const index = first + offset;
          return (
            <div
              key={index}
              role="listitem"
              data-index={index}
              // Without these, a screen reader reports the DOM it can see -
              // "item 3 of 20" - and the user has no idea the list has ten
              // thousand entries. Windowing without them trades a performance
              // problem for an accessibility one.
              aria-setsize={count}
              aria-posinset={index + 1}
              style={{
                position: "absolute",
                top: index * rowHeight,
                height: rowHeight,
                left: 0,
                right: 0,
              }}
            >
              {renderRow(item, index)}
            </div>
          );
        })}
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}

