## Challenge — the button

Edit `challenge/solution.tsx`. Tokens, variants, slots, theming and an override
path that survives.

Export:

```tsx
export const BUTTON_CSS: string;

export function Button<T extends ElementType = "button">(props: {
  as?: T;
  intent?: "primary" | "secondary" | "danger";
  size?: "sm" | "md" | "lg";
  loading?: boolean;
  startSlot?: ReactNode;
  endSlot?: ReactNode;
  className?: string;
  children: ReactNode;
} & Omit<ComponentPropsWithoutRef<T>, "as" | "className" | "children">): ReactElement;
```

Requirements:

- `BUTTON_CSS` uses **cascade layers**: `@layer components` for the button's own
  rules, and it must document — in a comment inside the string — which layer
  consumers should override in;
- every colour and every space value in `BUTTON_CSS` is a `var(--…)` reference,
  and each is written as a two-level lookup so a component-level token can
  override the semantic one — for example
  `background: var(--button-bg, var(--color-accent))`;
- variants are produced by the `variants()` function from ex5, with a compound
  variant for `intent="danger"` at `size="lg"`;
- state is published as `data-*` and ARIA, per ex6 — `data-loading`,
  `aria-busy`, `aria-disabled` — and the class attribute does not change with
  state;
- `startSlot` and `endSlot` are slots, rendered only when supplied, wrapped so
  they can be styled but never given content of the component's own;
- while `loading`, the button shows the `startSlot` replaced by a spinner
  element, keeps its width stable, is `aria-busy`, and does not fire `onClick`;
- polymorphic `as`, with `type="button"` applied only when it renders a
  `<button>`;
- a caller's `className` merges after the component's classes.

Honest difficulty note: no new idea after ex1–ex8. The hard requirement is the
override path — a caller must be able to change the padding with one class and
not lose the rest of the styling — and the reason it is hard is CSS specificity,
not React.

